<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tōdai-ji</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tōdai-ji is a Buddhist temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, built on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shomu">Emperor Shōmu</a> as the head of the state temples of the provinces. Its Great Buddha Hall houses a colossal bronze statue of the Buddha Vairocana, dedicated in 752. Rebuilt after fires, the hall is among the largest wooden buildings in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/todai-ji.jpg" alt="Tōdai-ji" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wiiii · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:T%C5%8Ddai-ji_Kon-d%C5%8D.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> suffered disasters and epidemics in the Tenpyō era, among them a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> outbreak around 735 to 737 and a rebellion led by Fujiwara no Hirotsugu in 740. In 741 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shomu">Emperor Shōmu</a> issued an edict promoting provincial temples throughout the country, and in 743 he commissioned the Great Buddha. Kinshōsen-ji, the forerunner of Tōdai-ji, was made the provincial temple of Yamato Province and head of all the provincial temples.</p><p>According to the temple's records, more than 2,600,000 people contributed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, wood, metal, cloth or labour, and 350,000 worked directly on the statue. It was cast in eight pours over three years, begun at Shigaraki and resumed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> in 745, and finished in 751. The eye-opening ceremony of 752 was attended by 10,000 monks and 4,000 dancers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Great Buddha Hall houses the Buddha Vairocana, known in Japanese as Daibutsu, said to be the world's largest bronze statue of him. The original complex also had two pagodas of about 100 metres, which an earthquake destroyed.</p><p>The hall has been rebuilt twice after fire. The present building, finished in 1709, is 57 metres long, 50 metres wide and 49 metres high, about 30 per cent smaller than its predecessor, which had eleven bays to its seven. It was the world's largest wooden building until 1998.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> period the state regulated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> closely, and Tōdai-ji served as the central administrative temple for the provincial temples and for the six Buddhist schools of the time, each of which had an office there. All officially licensed monks had to take Vinaya ordination at the temple. In 754 Ganjin, who had reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> after twelve years and six attempts to cross the sea from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, ordained Empress Kōken, the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shomu">Emperor Shōmu</a> and others.</p><p>The temple's authority declined when the centre of Japanese Buddhism moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hiei">Mount Hiei</a> and political power moved to the shōgun's base at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> after the Genpei war. By the 19th century the Great Buddha Hall was in danger of collapse under its heavy roof. A restoration from 1904 to 1913 propped it up, and a second, from 1974 to 1980, fixed the roof with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> frames and gave the hall its present appearance.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tōdai-ji” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Emperor Shomu built <b>Todai-ji</b> with its Great Buddha, dedicated in 752.</blockquote><a href="/state/ritsuryo-japan">Ritsuryo Japan</a></li></ul></section>
